This game here is meant as a recreation of an old mobile game called Jumpstun.

## Building

Requires CMake (3.11+) and a C compiler. raylib is downloaded automatically during configuration.

    cmake . -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5
    make
    ./spiel

Alternatively, `run.sh` cleans previous build files, rebuilds and starts the game.

## Controls

- Desktop: X / Space / click to jump, hold DOWN / S / right mouse button (or a side mouse button) to fall faster, arrow keys + X in menus, BACKSPACE to go back, M toggles music
- Android: tap to jump and to use the menus, the back button goes back

## Music and sound

The background music is chiptune synthesized at runtime in `src/audio.c` (no music files).
Each track is a few lines of note data: chords, a melody, a bass and a drum pattern.
Four tracks are included; "Sunny Hop" is free, the others can be bought in the shop.
The jump sound is generated the same way. Music can be switched off in the main menu.

## Twitch Plays

Press the TWITCH PLAY button in the main menu (both desktop and Android) and type a Twitch
channel name to connect. The game joins that channel's chat anonymously (read-only, no login
needed) and lets everyone currently chatting fly the chicken together: every half second it
tallies how many people typed "jump" vs. "fall"/"down" and acts on whichever is ahead. Since
chat has no real participant limit, this is how the game supports arbitrarily large groups -
including the thousands of viewers a stream can have - without needing a game server of our own.

## Android

The Android app lives in `android/` and shares the game code in `src/`.
Requires JDK 17+ and the Android SDK with `platforms;android-36`, `build-tools;36.0.0`,
`ndk;29.0.14206865` and `cmake;3.31.6`.

    cd android
    echo "sdk.dir=$HOME/Android/Sdk" > local.properties
    ./gradlew assembleDebug
    adb install -r app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk

The APK ends up in `android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/`. GitHub Actions also builds it on every push.

### Ads (Google AdMob)

The game offers optional rewarded videos: +10 coins in the shop and one revive per run.
A GDPR consent dialog (Google UMP) is shown where required, and a PRIVACY button in the
menu lets players change their choice.

Debug builds always use Google's test ads. For a release with real ads:

1. Create an app and a rewarded ad unit in AdMob and set up the GDPR message under
   "Privacy & messaging".
2. Put the IDs and your signing key into `~/.gradle/gradle.properties` (never commit them):

        jumperchicken.admobAppId=ca-app-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX~YYYYYYYYYY
        jumperchicken.admobRewardedId=ca-app-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/ZZZZZZZZZZ
        jumperchicken.keystore=/path/to/release.jks
        jumperchicken.keystorePassword=...
        jumperchicken.keyAlias=...
        jumperchicken.keyPassword=...

3. Build with `./gradlew bundleRelease` and upload the `.aab` to the Google Play Console.

Never tap your own real ads; AdMob bans accounts for that. Use the debug build for testing.

### Leaderboard (Google Play Games)

Scores are uploaded to a Google Play Games leaderboard, where players show up with their
Play Games name. The LEADERBOARD button in the menu only appears once it is configured:

1. In the Google Play Console, create the app, then under "Play Games Services > Setup and
   management" create the Play Games project and add an Android credential with the
   package name `io.github.bonbon08.jumperchicken` and the SHA-1 of your signing key
   (debug and release key, plus the Play App Signing key once the app is on Play).
2. Under "Leaderboards" create one leaderboard (score format: numeric, higher is better).
3. Add yourself as a tester, then put both IDs into `android/gradle.properties`
   (they are not secret):

        jumperchicken.playGamesAppId=123456789012
        jumperchicken.leaderboardId=CgkIxxxxxxxxxxxxEAIQAQ

The highscore is submitted after every run; scores from before sign-in are uploaded later.

## Difficulty

Every wall touch makes the chicken a bit faster (up to a cap), and every 3 wall touches is a
new level with one more spike (4 at the start, at most 7). The values are at the top of the
difficulty section in `src/objects.c`.
